# One historical finding, ignored by exact fingerprint.
#
# Commit 0c12315 added prisma/migrations/shipped.lock.json, which pinned the
# SHA-256 of each shipped migration as JSON. Gitleaks' generic-api-key rule reads
# a "name": "<64 hex>" pair as a key assignment and flagged it at entropy 3.87.
# It is a checksum of a file committed in the same repo -- public by construction,
# and useless to an attacker.
#
# The file was replaced in 2a9f899 with prisma/migrations/shipped.sha256 in
# sha256sum's own "<hash>  <path>" format, which is not an assignment and does not
# match the rule. But the scan walks the pull request's commit range, so the
# original commit is still read; deleting the file did not clear it, and history
# is not rewritten here (AGENTS.md 7: do not force-push).
#
# Scoped to that one commit, that one file, that one line. No rule is disabled, no
# path is exempted, and nothing future is covered -- a fingerprint names a commit
# that already exists. There is deliberately no .gitleaks.toml: the scan keeps the
# default ruleset, which the job log confirms on every run.
